WEBVTT

00:00:00.259 --> 00:00:01.920
Or consider something as ubiquitous

00:00:01.920 --> 00:00:03.300
as a shipment record.

00:00:03.519 --> 00:00:05.100
Okay. If you look at a

00:00:05.100 --> 00:00:07.680
standard supply chain database, a single

00:00:07.680 --> 00:00:09.840
shipment record might contain half

00:00:09.840 --> 00:00:11.280
a dozen different date fields.

00:00:11.640 --> 00:00:13.620
Oh yeah, order date, ship date,

00:00:13.620 --> 00:00:14.199
all of that.

00:00:14.300 --> 00:00:16.300
Right. You have the original order

00:00:16.300 --> 00:00:18.180
date, the warehouse processing date,

00:00:18.280 --> 00:00:19.980
the physical ship date, the initial

00:00:19.980 --> 00:00:22.259
estimated arrival date, the revised

00:00:22.259 --> 00:00:24.240
arrival date based on, say, weather

00:00:24.240 --> 00:00:26.760
delays, and the actual docked date.

00:00:26.980 --> 00:00:27.980
That is a lot of dates.

00:00:28.580 --> 00:00:30.220
So if an executive asks a

00:00:30.220 --> 00:00:32.200
standard AI chatbot, hey, when is

00:00:32.200 --> 00:00:33.300
the inventory arriving?

00:00:33.900 --> 00:00:34.940
How does the language model know

00:00:34.940 --> 00:00:36.580
which of those six columns to

00:00:36.580 --> 00:00:38.779
pull? Usually it just guesses, right?

00:00:38.879 --> 00:00:39.800
Or it hallucinates.

00:00:40.680 --> 00:00:42.519
Historically, a human data engineer

00:00:42.519 --> 00:00:44.220
has had to explicitly write SQL

00:00:44.220 --> 00:00:45.880
queries to tell the reporting system

00:00:45.880 --> 00:00:47.920
which of those fields represents the

00:00:47.920 --> 00:00:50.480
actual expected arrival that the store

00:00:50.480 --> 00:00:52.279
manager cares about today,
